@startuml proto-servo-timing
' ===========================================================================
' proto-servo-timing.puml - (c) servo PWM wire timing, in real numbers.
'
' Measured by tb/tb_pe_soc_servo.v on real RTL (60 MHz, Icarus, the real SRAM
' macro), firmware/servo_sweep.pe:
'
'   pulse 0:  1000.15 us   (+0.15 vs the 1000 us requested)
'   pulse 1:  1500.13 us   (+0.13)
'   pulse 2:  1750.12 us   (+0.12)
'   pulse 3:  1250.13 us   (+0.13)
'   pulse 4:  2000.10 us   (+0.10)
'   frame period (1000 us -> 1500 us): 19999.95 us = 50.000 Hz
'   dmem[10] (position index) = 5
'
' Every one of those five is +0.10 to +0.15 us LONG, and the direction is
' systematic rather than noise: the delay routine's count is exact and the
' residue is the table entry's fitting, which the firmware's own header states
' is bounded at 0.25 us.
' ===========================================================================

title Servo PWM - wire timing\n<font size=12>measured: 1000.15 / 1500.13 / 1750.12 / 1250.13 / 2000.10 us, frame 19 999.95 us = 50.000 Hz</font>

hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #33555
  LifeLineBorderColor #33555
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  ParticipantBorderColor #33555
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
  ParticipantFontName monospaced
  ActorFontName monospaced
  NoteFontName monospaced
  GroupFontName monospaced
}

participant "CPU\npe_cpu.v\nsingle-cycle" as CPU
participant "pad 6\nTXPIN / PINOE" as PAD
participant "servo signal\nidle LOW" as WIRE

== claiming the pin ==

CPU -> PAD : PINOE = 0x00 (release every pad)
CPU -> PAD : TXPIN = 0x00  (the LEVEL first...)
note right of PAD
  ...and the idle level is set BEFORE the
  pin is claimed. A first version wrote
  SRV_DATA here instead of 0, on the reasoning
  that the pin was about to go high anyway.

  The consequence: the line stayed RELEASED -
  so pulled HIGH by the board's pull-up - from
  reset until the first pulse, and the first
  pulse therefore had <b>no rising edge at all</b>.
  The TB saw four rises and five falls and
  correctly refused to call it five pulses.

  <b>The idle level of a timing protocol is part
  of the protocol.</b>
end note
CPU -> PAD : PINOE = 0x40 (claim exactly one pad)
PAD -> WIRE : the line is released, so it READS HIGH for a moment
note over WIRE
  This is the TB's "leading fall": the wire model
  is a pull-up plus the pads, so a released pin
  floats up. It is why the TB pairs each RISE with
  the NEXT fall rather than with the j-th fall.
end note
PAD -> WIRE : the firmware drives it LOW: the servo idle

== position 0: 1 000 us, then a FULL 20 ms slot ==

CPU -> PAD : TXPIN = SRV_DATA (0x40) - the pulse starts
note over WIRE
  <b>PULSE 0 = 1 000.15 us high</b>
  requested 1 000 us, <b>+0.15</b>
  datasheet band: 1 000 .. 2 000 us
end note
note over CPU
  dly1/dly2/dly3 on dmem[0] = 97, (n2,n3) = (2,152)

  total = (n1-1)*(10 + (n2-1)*(4*n3+7)) + 4
  one outer step = 10 + 1*(4*152 + 7) = 625 clocks = <b>10.4167 us</b>
  96 * 625 + 4 = 60 004 clocks = 1 000.07 us

  The tables are FITTED by division and a small
  search, not by trial and error: for a chosen
  (n2,n3) the outer counter nearest the target is
  a division, and keeping every entry inside 255
  is the search.

  <b>The step is 10 + (n2-1)*(4*n3+7), not 4*n3+11.</b>
  The shorter form misses the outer loop's six
  instructions and the four that return it: 619
  clocks instead of 625, and a reader who
  multiplies it out gets 990 us and concludes the
  measured 1 000.15 us is a percent out.
end note
CPU -> PAD : TXPIN = 0x00 - the gap starts
note over WIRE
  <b>GAP 0 = 19.000 ms</b>  (dmem[5] = 229, (11,123))
  one outer step = 10 + 10*(4*123 + 7) = 5 000 clocks = 83.3333 us
  228 * 5000 + 4 = 1 140 004 clocks = 19 000.07 us
  rise 0 -> rise 1 = <b>19 999.95 us = 50.000 Hz</b>
  this is the number the 50 Hz claim rests on
end note

== position 1: 1 500 us, and the frame period is MEASURED here ==

CPU -> PAD : TXPIN = SRV_DATA (0x40)
note over WIRE
  <b>PULSE 1 = 1 500.13 us high</b>
  requested 1 500 us, <b>+0.13</b>
end note
CPU -> PAD : TXPIN = 0x00
note over WIRE
  <b>GAP 1 = 18.500 ms</b>  (dmem[6] = 223)

  The frame period is measured across TWO
  DIFFERENT pulse widths on purpose. A fixed gap
  after each pulse would give 20.5 ms here and
  21.0 ms at the next position - 49 Hz then
  47.6 Hz - and every per-pulse WIDTH check would
  still pass. This is the measurement that tells
  a slot from a delay.
end note

== positions 2..4: the widths still have to be right ==

group short sweep gaps - 2.5 ms each, not slots
  CPU -> PAD : TXPIN = SRV_DATA / 0x00, 3 x (pulse, gap)
  note over WIRE
    <b>1750.12</b> (+0.12) then 4 250.58 us\n
    <b>1250.13</b> (+0.13) then 3 750.60 us\n
    <b>2000.10</b> (+0.10) then (short)\n
    Each short gap still exceeds 1 000 us, and the
    TB PRINTS them as "a short sweep gap, not a
    frame" rather than reporting them as slots.
  end note
end group

== done ==

CPU -> PAD : dmem[10] = 5
note over WIRE
  The firmware parks with the line LOW, and the
  TB asserts the LAST recorded edge is a fall.
  A firmware that parked high would leave the
  servo held at full travel forever.
end note

== the tolerances, and why they are the ones they are ==

note over CPU, WIRE
  <b>Bands the TB uses, and what each is protecting.</b>
  ...
  pulse width      1 000 .. 2 001 us   the servo would ignore or saturate outside
  vs requested     +/- 30 us            the sweep would not be a sweep
  frame period     19 900 .. 20 100 us  49.5 .. 50.25 Hz - the motor hums outside
  short gap        > 1 000 us           a short gap is still a gap, not a second pulse
  ...
  <b>PULSE_MAX_US is 2000 + 1, and the 1 is stated rather than hidden:</b> a
  firmware that produced exactly 2000.0 us would otherwise sit ON the datasheet
  ceiling, and a ceiling a program can land on exactly is a ceiling a rounding
  error can push it over.
  ...
  <b>Why the frame period is a band and the pulse widths are checked against
  the REQUEST.</b> A frame period is two measured widths added together, so its
  error is the sum of the two tables' fitting error - bounded at 0.25 us each by
  the construction in the firmware header. A band is the honest shape of that
  claim. The widths are compared against what the firmware was ASKED for, which
  is a different and stronger statement: it pins the sweep, not just the band.
end note

note over CPU, WIRE
  <b>The five measured widths are all 0.10 to 0.15 us LONG, and that is systematic.</b> The delay routine's clock count is exact - the core
  is single-cycle, so N instructions take exactly N clocks - and the residue is
  the table entry's FITTING: the outer counter is a whole number of steps, so
  each entry lands at or just above its target. A count that could land short as
  easily as long would be a worse mechanism, because the datasheet's interesting
  bound on this protocol is a MINIMUM.
end note

@enduml
